<h3>My Recommendations:</h3>
<p><strong>For Reels/Shorts:</strong> CapCut. The auto-captions, trend templates, and speed ramping tools are designed for vertical content. I edit 90% of my Instagram Reels in CapCut on my phone — it takes 15-20 minutes for a finished Reel.</p>

<p><strong>For longer YouTube videos on phone:</strong> VN Video Editor. It's genuinely powerful — multi-track timeline, keyframe animations, speed curves, color correction. And it's completely free with no watermark. Most people don't know about VN, which is a shame because it's better than KineMaster in almost every way.</p>

<p><strong>For iPad users:</strong> LumaFusion or DaVinci Resolve iPad. LumaFusion is the "Final Cut Pro of iPad" — lightweight, fast, professional output. DaVinci Resolve iPad is more powerful but heavier on system resources.</p>

<h2>4. The Phone-First Shooting Workflow</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>Plan your shots</strong> before hitting record. Even a mental shot list helps.</li>
<li><strong>Shoot in landscape</strong> for YouTube, portrait for Reels/Shorts. Simple but many forget.</li>
<li><strong>Film 20% more than you think you need.</strong> It's free — storage is cheap. Having extra footage in edit saves reshoots.</li>
<li><strong>Get close.</strong> Phone sensors are small. The closer you are to your subject, the better the depth-of-field separation and the less digital noise.</li>
<li><strong>Clean your lens.</strong> Seriously. The smudged lens from your pocket ruins sharpness and creates haze. Wipe it before every shot.</li>
<li><strong>Use external audio.</strong> Phone mics are terrible for anything beyond arm's length. A wireless lav (DJI Mic Mini at ₹5,000, or BOYA BY-WM3 at ₹2,500) transforms your production value instantly.</li>
</ol>

