<h2>FAQ</h2>

<details><summary>Can I make professional YouTube videos on just a phone?</summary>
<p>Yes — with good lighting, clean audio (external mic), and a solid editing workflow. MKBHD shot an entire video on an iPhone to prove this point. The content, audio quality, and editing matter far more than whether you used a phone or a ₹3 lakh cinema camera. Start with what you have.</p>
</details>

<details><summary>Is CapCut safe to use?</summary>
<p>CapCut is owned by ByteDance (TikTok's parent). It collects usage data. For personal social media content, this is generally fine. For sensitive corporate content, some companies restrict ByteDance apps. Use your judgment. Read our <a href="/best-free-video-editing-software-beginners-2026">full software comparison</a> for alternatives.</p>
</details>

<details><summary>Phone vs Camera — should I buy a camera or upgrade my phone?</summary>
<p>If your primary content is Reels/Shorts/vlogs: a flagship phone (iPhone 15 Pro, Samsung S24) is better value than an entry camera. If you want cinematic depth-of-field, interchangeable lenses, LOG footage, and professional audio — get a camera. Most people should start with their phone and upgrade to a camera when they hit the phone's limitations.</p>
</details>

<details><summary>How do I transfer phone footage to my desktop for editing?</summary>
<p>Fastest methods: USB-C cable (direct transfer), AirDrop (iPhone → Mac), Google Drive/OneDrive upload (slower but wireless). For large files (4K ProRes), USB cable is the only practical option. Cloud upload of a 5-minute 4K ProRes clip would take hours on Indian internet speeds.</p>
</details>

